== Love That Purifies the Unclean

=== §. 34. #emph[Of Christ’s grace that goes before (his preventing grace).]

In setting down how the condition of the church named before is changed, note:

+ The #emph[manner] of setting it forth.
+ The #emph[matter,] or substance, of it.

The #emph[manner] is implied in this conjunction#footnote[ἵνα ἁγιάσῃ.] THAT (“that He might sanctify her”). Christ loved the church and gave himself up for her, not because she was sanctified, but “that He might sanctify her.” This shows that

#emph[The grace which Christ shows to the church is a grace that goes before]#footnote[Doctrine.] #emph[(a preventing grace).] Sanctification is no cause but an effect of Christ’s#footnote[#emph[Ipse nos gratis saluos fecit, ipse nos non eum quaerentes quaesiuit, inuenit, etc.] \[He saved us freely; he sought us when we were not seeking him, and found us, etc.\] #emph[Aug. in Psal.] 78.] love, and it follows in order after his love. His love arose only and wholly from himself. In the parties loved there was nothing but matter for hatred before they were loved. Moses says this of the love of God to Israel: “The #smallcaps[Lord] did not set His love on you#super[+] … because you were more numerous …, but because the #smallcaps[Lord] loved you.” At first sight this may seem to be (as#footnote[#emph[Non est inuenire Domino sed praeuenire.] \[With the Lord it is not to find, but to go before.\] #emph[Bern. serm.] 78. #emph[in Cant.]] we say)#footnote[Idem per idem. \[The same by the same.\]] #emph[a woman’s reason:] that the Lord should set his love on them because he loved them. But when it is duly considered, we shall find the ground of God’s love excellently set forth here: it rests altogether in himself, and in his own good pleasure.#footnote[Deut 7:7, 8.] Indeed, since this is noted as the #emph[end] of Christ’s love, “that He might sanctify her,” it further shows that it was not any #emph[foresight] of holiness in the church that moved him to love it. First he loved it, and then he sought how to make it lovely and worthy to be loved.

In this Christ’s love differs from the love of all men toward#footnote[The difference between Christ’s love and man’s. Esth 2:12, #emph[etc.]] their spouses. For they must see something in them to move them to love. When Ahasuerus was to choose a wife, the maidens from whom he was to take one were first purified, and then he took the one in whom he most delighted. But Christ first loves his spouse, and then sanctifies her. Before he loved her, he saw nothing in her for which he should prefer her before the world.

Seeing that all the beauty#footnote[Use.] and dignity of the church is “from Him and through Him and to Him,” “to Him be the glory forever!#footnote[Rom 11:36.] Amen.”

=== §. 35. #emph[Of Christ’s seeking to make his church pure.]

The #emph[matter,] or substance, of that subordinate #emph[end] which Christ aimed at in giving himself up for the church is in these words (“that He might sanctify her, having cleansed her”). In general they show that

#emph[Christ seeks the purity of his church.] For this end he has#footnote[Doctrine.] shed his own most pure and precious blood (for his blood “cleanses us from all sin”)#footnote[1 John 1:7.] and conveyed his Holy Spirit into his body, the church. The Spirit is called the #emph[Spirit of sanctification,]#footnote[Rom 1:4.] because he renews and sanctifies those in whom he is.

Christ aims at this: that he might make his spouse like#footnote[Reason.] himself, pure as he is pure.

We who profess ourselves#footnote[1 #emph[Use.] Exhortation. 1 John 3:3.] to be of this church must strive after that end which Christ aimed at. For “everyone who has this hope in Him purifies himself, just as Christ is pure.” Let us therefore use all good means to cleanse ourselves from all filthiness of flesh and spirit.

Since this is the end which Christ aims at for the good#footnote[2 #emph[Use.] Trial.] of his church, to #emph[cleanse it,] those who find themselves cleansed have good evidence that they are of this church. Those who are not cleansed can have no assurance of it.

How unworthy of this benefit are those who live as the#footnote[3 #emph[Use.] Reproof.] world does, and like swine wallow in the mire at every occasion, being drawn into sin by every temptation! Do they not, as much as lies in them, make the death of Christ to be in vain, and pervert that main end which Christ aimed at in giving himself up?

But what may be thought of those who, like Ishmael, mock and scoff at those who labor to be cleansed?

=== §. 36. #emph[Of the church’s justification.]

The two particular parts of the end named before, #emph[cleansing] and #emph[sanctifying,] set forth more distinctly the purity of the church even in this world. #emph[Cleansing] has relation to the blood of Christ, and so points out our #emph[justification.]

#emph[Sanctifying] has relation to the Spirit of Christ, who works our sanctification.

From this cleansing of the church meant here, I gather that #emph[no sin lies upon the church.] For the blood of Christ “cleanses#footnote[1 John 1:7.]#footnote[Doctrine.] us from all sin.” This is to be taken of the guilt of sin, which is clean taken away by Christ’s death. So the sin which is in us is as if it were not in us, because it is not imputed to us.

See here the blessed condition of the church: “Blessed is#footnote[Use.] he whose transgressions are forgiven, whose sins are covered. Blessed#footnote[Ps 32:1, 2.] is the man whose iniquity the #smallcaps[Lord] does not impute to him.”

=== §. 37. #emph[Of the church’s sanctification.]

From the sanctifying of the church mentioned here, I further gather that

#emph[The church is made holy and righteous.] This is meant here of#footnote[Doctrine.] that inherent righteousness which the Spirit of Christ works in all the members of his body. In this respect they are called #emph[saints.]#footnote[1 Cor 1:2.] So not only is the guilt of sin taken away, but the very “body of sin” is also so destroyed in them#footnote[Rom 6:6.] that it can no longer reign in them, nor can they obey it in its lusts. Instead of the dominion of sin, the Spirit of Christ reigns in them, and leads them into all righteousness.

See here the free condition of the church. Whereas the#footnote[Use.] world lies under the slavery of sin and the tyranny of Satan, the church is “set free from sin” and has become a slave “to righteousness”;#footnote[Rom 6:18, 11.] it is “dead to sin, but alive to God in Christ Jesus.”

=== §. 38. #emph[Of the church’s purity before God and man.]

From the joining together of these two benefits of Christ’s death, justification and sanctification, we see that #emph[the church is both spotless before God and blameless before men.] The blood of Christ so cleanses her that in God’s sight she has no spot of sin. And the Spirit of Christ so sanctifies her that her righteousness shines before men. For “the grace of God”#footnote[Titus 2:11, 12.] “instructs us to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions, and to live sensible, upright, and godly lives in the present age.” In this respect the church is said to be “all glorious within,” and her#footnote[Ps 45:13.] “gown” also to be “embroidered with gold.” And Zechariah and Elizabeth,#footnote[Luke 1:6.] members of this church, are said to be “righteous in the sight of God” and “blameless,” namely before men. There is no such purity in any as in the church. For true and perfect beauty#footnote[#emph[Vera & perfecta pulchritudo in nullo hominum est nisi in corpore Christi quod interpretatur Ecclesia. Hier. in Ezech.] 27.] is only in the body of Christ, which is the church, of which it is said, “You are altogether beautiful … there is no spot in you” (Song 4:7).

1\. #emph[Question.] Is it possible that neither God nor man should spy any fault in those who are of the true church, while they live here in this world?

#emph[Answer.] Seeing that the flesh remains in the best while they remain#footnote[Sin is in the best.] in the world, it cannot be but that both God and man must spy many blemishes in the best. “Everything is#footnote[Heb 4:13.] uncovered and exposed before the eyes of Him.” If therefore any remnant of sin is in the saints (as there are exceedingly many in every one, so that “if we say we have no sin, we deceive ourselves,#footnote[1 John 1:8.] and the truth is not in us”), it is without question plain in his sight. Indeed, such is the imperfection and weakness of the best saints that the flesh, always lusting in them against the Spirit, often prevails, and so shows itself in some evil fruit or other, so that the eye of man spies it. Take as instances the examples of the best who ever lived in any age.

2\. #emph[Question.] How then are they spotless before God and#footnote[How the saints are spotless before God.] blameless before men?

#emph[Answer.] 1. God so fully discharges and acquits the church of all her sins that in his account she is as if she had no speck of sin at all. In this respect David uses the metaphor of #emph[covering sin,]#footnote[Ps 32:1, 2.] and explains his meaning by these two phrases, #emph[forgiving] and #emph[not imputing sin.]

2\. It is the course of a man’s life, not this or that particular#footnote[How the saints are blameless before men. #emph[Vna hirundo non facit ver.] \[One swallow does not make a spring.\]] action, which makes a man blameworthy or blameless; just as it is the flock of swallows, and not one here or another there, which shows the spring. Now because the constant conduct of those who are of the church is blameless before men, they may justly be accounted so, even though some particular blameworthy things at times pass from them. See here how the true saints may boldly lift up their faces before God and man. The soundness of their faith gives them confidence before God. The testimony of their conscience gives them courage before men. Let all who desire this boldness join a sound faith and a good conscience together, and labor for assurance both of their cleansing by the blood of Christ and of their sanctifying by the Spirit of Christ.

=== §. 39. #emph[Of the order and dependence of justification and sanctification on one another.]

The order and manner of knitting these two benefits together is worth noting.

The letter puts sanctification in the first place, but the sense presupposes justification. For this is how he says it: “that He might sanctify her,#footnote[καθαρίσας.] having cleansed her.” The #emph[cleansing] spoken of here is an inward, invisible work, and the evidence of it is sanctification, which is an outward work, perceived by the senses. Therefore #emph[this] is expressed first, and then #emph[that] is inferred, as a thing that must necessarily be presupposed.

From this arise these doctrines. Our English, with this word #emph[having,] fitly and properly renders the Greek active participles of the past (the preterperfect or finite) tenses. Because the Latin lacks these, Latin translators are forced to use the passive, or a periphrasis (a roundabout phrase): as #emph[vt illam sanctificaret mundatam] \[that he might sanctify her, cleansed\], Erasmus; #emph[Postquam eam purgasset] \[after he had cleansed her\], Beza.

1\. #emph[In order, justification goes before#footnote[1\. #emph[Doctrine. Iustificatio non accedit factoribus sed factores legis praecedit.] \[Justification does not come upon those who are doers, but goes before the doers of the law.\] #emph[Aug. de Spir. & lit. cap.] 26.] sanctification.] I say “in order,” because at the very moment that Christ cleanses his church by his blood, he begins to sanctify her. But when he begins to sanctify her, he has already cleansed her: she is justified.

The grace of justification, then, is a most free grace. It is not wrought upon any righteousness of ours, for it comes before it.

2\. #emph[Christ sanctifies those whom he has cleansed.] The#footnote[2\. #emph[Doctrine.]] apostle proves this at length in the sixth chapter of Romans. Let none therefore boast of their cleansing by Christ’s blood until they find themselves renewed and sanctified by the Spirit of Christ. For note the apostle’s description of those who are justified by Christ, which for more clearness may be set down by question and answer. For whom is there no condemnation?#footnote[Rom 8:1.] For those who are in Christ Jesus. Who are they? Those “who do not walk according to the flesh but according to the Spirit.”

3\. #emph[Sanctification presupposes justification.] Those who are sanctified#footnote[3\. #emph[Doctrine.]] may rest on this, that they are cleansed and justified.

For sanctification is a fruit of justification. In this respect James says that “we are justified by works,”#footnote[Jas 2:24.] that is, declared to be justified.

Wonderful is the comfort which the saints in this world#footnote[Use.] reap from this. For their sanctification is imperfect, and the flesh abides in them and lusts against the Spirit. Indeed, sin is present with them when they would do good, so that they are often forced to complain and cry, “What wretched men we are!#footnote[Rom 7:24.] Who will rescue us from this body of death?” If they had no other ground on which to fasten the anchor of their hope but their sanctification, it could not hold them fast enough against the storms of Satan’s temptations. But because their sanctification is a fruit and evidence of their justification, they take heart, and thank God that with the mind they themselves serve the law of God, though with the flesh they serve the law of sin. And so, upheld and comforted, they go on striving against sin until it is clean rooted out of them, as well as forgiven.

=== §. 40. #emph[Of the sacramental washing of water.]

One of the means which Christ uses for the cleansing and sanctifying of his church is expressed under this phrase: “with the washing of water.” Water is the outward element used in baptism; #emph[washing] is the principal sacramental rite in it. #emph[Water] sets forth Christ’s blood; #emph[washing] points out its application and efficacy, which is the purging and cleansing of our souls. As water without washing makes nothing clean, so the blood of Christ, without a right application of it, cleanses no man’s soul.

Since this #emph[washing of water] mentioned here is applied to an inward, spiritual cleansing, what else can it set forth but the sacrament of baptism, in which both #emph[water] and #emph[washing] are used?

#emph[Objection.] There is but little washing used in the sacrament of baptism, nothing but sprinkling a little water on the face of the one who is baptized.

#emph[Answer.] That sprinkling is enough to show the use of water.#footnote[Why water in baptism is only sprinkled.] The one to be baptized is not brought to the font to have his face, or any other part of his body, made clean, but to have assurance of the inward cleansing of his soul. Now, so that our minds may not fasten too much on the outward thing done, but be wholly raised up to the mystery, the outward element is used no further than may serve to put us in mind of the inward thing signified by it. In the same way, in the Lord’s Supper not so much bread and wine is given and received as would satisfy one’s appetite, or slake his hunger and quench his thirst. Only a little bit of bread and a taste of wine are given, to declare the use of bread and wine, and so to draw the minds of the communicants to consider their spiritual nourishment by the body and blood of Jesus Christ.

=== §. 41. #emph[How baptism is a means of cleansing and sanctifying.]

The manner of joining this sacramental washing to the sanctifying and cleansing of the church, “with the washing of water,” shows that

#emph[Baptism is a means of sanctifying and cleansing the church.] All#footnote[Doctrine.] those places of Scripture that ascribe #emph[regeneration,]#footnote[John 3:5. Titus 3:5.] #emph[justification,]#footnote[Gal 3:27.] #emph[sanctification,]#footnote[Rom 6:3.] or #emph[salvation]#footnote[1 Pet 3:21.] to it prove as much. But so that the truth of it may be understood more fully and distinctly, I will briefly show:

+ In what respect baptism is a means of our sanctifying and cleansing.
+ What kind of means it is.
+ How necessary it is.

It may be said to be a means, as stated above, in four special respects.

1\. In that it most vividly represents and sets forth, even to the outward senses, the inward cleansing of our souls by the blood of Christ, and the sanctifying of us by the Spirit of Christ. Apply the use of water (by washing in which foul things are made very clean) to the virtue of Christ’s blood and the efficacy of his Spirit, and the truth of this will plainly appear. For better help in this application, read Romans 6:4, etc.

2\. In that it truly sets forth and makes a tender, or offer, of the grace of justification and sanctification to the one baptized. In this respect it is described as “a baptism of repentance#footnote[Luke 3:3.] for the forgiveness of sins.”#footnote[Acts 2:38.] And Peter says to the same purpose, “Repent and be baptized, every one of you#super[+], … for the forgiveness of your sins.”

3\. In that it really exhibits (that is, holds out and gives) and seals up to the conscience of the one who is baptized the graces named before, by which he is assured that he is made partaker of them. So Abraham “received the sign of circumcision as a seal of the righteousness that he had#footnote[Rom 4:11.] by faith.” That is why the eunuch and others, when they were#footnote[Acts 8:39; 16:34.] baptized, went on their way rejoicing.

4\. In that it is a particular pledge, belonging to the one baptized, that he himself is made partaker of those graces. Therefore every one in particular is baptized for himself; indeed, though many are brought to the font at once, yet every one is baptized by name. To this purpose the apostle says, “Whoever has been baptized into Christ has clothed himself with Christ”:#footnote[Gal 3:27.] #emph[whoever,] whether Peter, John, Thomas, or any other particular person. Ananias said to Paul in the singular number, “Be#footnote[Acts 22:16.] baptized, and wash your sins away.”

=== §. 42. #emph[Objections against the efficacy of baptism answered.]

1\. #emph[Objection.] Many who are baptized receive no such grace at all. They are neither cleansed nor sanctified.

#emph[Answer.] They are only outwardly washed with water; they are not baptized with the Holy Spirit. The fault is not that no grace accompanies that sacrament, but that they do not receive, but reject, the grace which belongs to it. “What if some did not have faith? Will their lack of faith nullify#footnote[Rom 3:3.] God’s faithfulness? Certainly not!”

2\. #emph[Objection.] Many receive the graces named before before they are baptized, as Abraham did before he was circumcised,#footnote[Rom 4:11.] and such as were baptized after they believed. How then is baptism a means of them?

#emph[Answer.] Their spiritual cleansing is more vividly and fully shown by it, and they are the more assured of it.

3\. #emph[Objection.] Many who for a long time after their baptism have lived like swine in sin, and so have not been cleansed or sanctified, have yet been effectually called many years afterward. What means has baptism been of this?

#emph[Answer.] The use and efficacy of baptism is not passing, as the act of it is, but lasting and perpetual, as long as the one baptized lives. Whenever a sinner sincerely repents and lays hold in faith on the promises of God, baptism, which is the seal of them, is as powerful and effectual as it could have been when it was first administered. For the efficacy of baptism consists in the free offer of grace. As long, therefore, as God continues to offer grace, so long may a man’s baptism be effectual. On this ground we are baptized only once for all. And as the#footnote[Jer 4:4.] prophets put the people in mind of their circumcision, so the apostles put them in mind of their baptism long after it was administered. Indeed, they speak of it (though the act was long past) as if it were being done in the present time: “baptism … now saves you#super[+].”#footnote[1 Pet 3:21.]

=== §. 43. #emph[What kind of means of grace baptism is.]

#smallcaps[II]. Baptism is no physical or natural means of working#footnote[#emph[Aqua exhibet forinsecus Sacramentum gratiae, & Spiritus operatur intrinsecus beneficium gratiae.] \[The water presents outwardly the sacrament of grace, and the Spirit works inwardly the benefit of grace.\] #emph[Aug. Epist.] 23.] grace, as if the grace which is sealed up by it were inherent in the water, or in the minister’s act of sprinkling it. (In medicines, salves, herbs, foods and the like, the virtue which comes from their use is inherent in them; when they are applied they have their effect, whether a man believes it or not.) Rather, it is only a voluntary instrument which Christ uses, as it pleases him, to work what grace, or what measure of#footnote[#emph[In Baptismo praecipua pars est Spiritus, per quem & aqua sit efficax.] \[In baptism the chief part is the Spirit, through whom the water also is made effectual.\] #emph[Chrys. in Act. Hom.] 1.] grace, seems best to him. So grace only attends it; it is not enclosed in it. Yet in the right use of it, Christ by his Spirit works that grace which is received by it. In this respect the minister is said to “baptize with water,” but Christ “with the Holy Spirit and with fire” (Matt 3:11).

=== §. 44. #emph[Of the necessity of baptism.]

#smallcaps[III]. A means of working a thing may be said to be necessary#footnote[Things are necessary in two ways.] in two ways.

1\. #emph[Absolutely,] so that the thing cannot possibly be without it.#footnote[Ex Hypothesi.] In this way the proper causes of a thing are absolutely necessary; as in this case God’s covenant, Christ’s blood, and the working of the Spirit are absolutely necessary for attaining any grace.

2\. #emph[By consequence,] so that, according to the course and order which God has set down, things cannot be without them.

Baptism is not absolutely necessary as a cause. For then it would be equal to God’s covenant, Christ’s blood, and the work of the Spirit. #emph[Indeed, then all who are baptized, without any exception, would be cleansed.]

But it is necessary #emph[by consequence,] and that in two#footnote[Ex hypothesi. \[By supposition.\]] respects:

+ In regard of God’s ordinance.
+ In regard of our need of it.

1\. Since God has ordained this as a sacrament to be used, it is necessary that it should be used, if for no other end, yet to show our obedience. He who carelessly neglects, or willfully despises, any sacrament which God commands him to use, “that soul will be cut off.”#footnote[Gen 17:14.]

2\. Great is the need that we have of it, in regard of our dullness in understanding spiritual things, and of our weakness in believing invisible things. We are carnal and earthly, and we understand spiritual and heavenly things better by things that are earthly and perceived by the senses. Therefore God has ordained visible elements to be sacraments of invisible graces. Again, we are slow to believe such things as are promised in the word. Therefore, the more to help and strengthen our faith, God has added to his covenant in the word his seal in and by the sacrament, so “that by two unchangeable things” (God’s covenant and God’s seal) “in which it is impossible for God to lie, we … may be strongly encouraged.” Besides, though in general we do believe the truth of God’s word, yet we are doubtful about applying it to ourselves. So, for the better applying of God’s covenant to our own souls, God has added his sacrament to his word.

=== §. 45. #emph[Of the contrary extremes of papists and Anabaptists about the necessity and efficacy of baptism.]

There are two extremes contrary to the points about baptism set down above.

One is in the excess: that of the papists, who ascribe too much to it, and make it a plain idol.

The other is in the defect: that of the Anabaptists and libertines, who#footnote[Papists ascribe too much to baptism. #emph[Bellarm. de Bapt lib.] 1. #emph[cap.] 4.] take too much from it, and make it an idle ceremony.

The papists go too far in two things: in the necessity and in the efficacy of baptism.

They make it so absolutely necessary that if any die unbaptized, he cannot be saved. They pass this sentence on infants, even though they are deprived of it without any fault of their own, or even of their parents, being stillborn. It is a merciless sentence without any warrant from God’s word; indeed, it is against his word and against the order which he has prescribed. He has established#footnote[Gen 17:7.] his covenant, and promised to be the God of the faithful and of their seed. On this ground Peter says, “This promise belongs to#footnote[Acts 2:39.] you#super[+] and your children.” And Paul says of “your#super[+] children” that “now they are holy.”#footnote[1 Cor 7:14.] Shall all these privileges be made void by a lack#footnote[Gen 17:12. Josh 5:5.] of baptism that could not be helped? If so, would God have commanded circumcision (which was to the Jews what baptism is to Christians) to be put off to the eighth day, before which day many infants died? Or would Moses have allowed it to be left undone all the time that the Israelites were in the wilderness? If it is said that baptism is more necessary than circumcision, I answer that the Scripture lays no more necessity on it. If it were as necessary as they make it, then the virtue of Christ’s death would be less effectual since he was actually manifested than it was before. For before, it was effectual for infants without a sacrament, but now, it seems, it is not. Had the ancient churches thought so of the absolute necessity of baptism, they would not have had set times for administering it, nor would they have allowed it to be put off as long as they did.#footnote[#emph[In Thessalia mos fuit vt paschalibus tantùm diebus baptismus perageretur,] \[In Thessaly the custom was that baptism was performed only on the days of Easter,\] #emph[Niceph. hist. lib.] 12. #emph[cap.] 34. #emph[Socrat. hist. lib.] 5. #emph[cap.] 22.] Some churches appointed it to be administered only at Easter.#footnote[Ne quenquam Sacerdotes baptizent praeterquam in Pascha & Pentecoste. \[Let the priests baptize no one except at Easter and Pentecost.\] L L. Pipin. Carol. M. etc.] Some appointed it at Easter and Pentecost. And though many who gave evidence of their true faith died before they were baptized, yet#footnote[Iustus quacunque morte praeuentus fuerit anima eius in refrigerio erit, dixit Ambr. Orat. de obit. Valent. Qui diem obijt antequam baptisaretur. \[“The righteous man, by whatever death he is overtaken, his soul will be at rest,” said Ambrose in his oration on the death of Valentinian, who died before he was baptized.\]] they did not on that account judge them to be damned. This practice and judgment of the ancients has made#footnote[#emph[Rhem. annot.] on John 3:5.] many papists somewhat soften that absolute necessity, and say that “in this case God, who has not bound his grace to any sacrament, in respect of his own freedom, may and does accept as baptized those who either are martyred before they could be baptized, or else depart this life with a vow and desire to have that sacrament, but by some necessity that could not be remedied could not obtain it.” If a necessity that cannot be remedied can help the matter, what necessity is so beyond remedy as for a child to be stillborn?

Again, they add such efficacy to baptism that it gives grace #emph[by the work itself,]#footnote[Ex opere operato, Rhem. loc. citat.] that is, by the very work done. In this they make it equal to the very blood of Christ, and take away the proper work of the Spirit, and the use of faith, repentance, and such graces. What can there be more in the water of baptism than there was in the blood of the beasts that were offered up in sacrifice? But “it is impossible for the blood of bulls and goats to take away sins.”#footnote[Heb 10:4.] They themselves ascribe no such efficacy to the word preached, and yet they cannot show where the Holy Spirit has given more virtue to baptism than to the word. This text joins them both together (“that He might … cleanse her with#footnote[1 Cor 1:21. Rom 1:16.] the washing of water by the word”). What more can be said of a means than what is said of the word? “God was pleased through … what was preached to save those who believe.” “The gospel … is the power of God for salvation,” etc.

On the other side, the Anabaptists and such libertines esteem#footnote[Anabaptists make too little of baptism.] this holy and necessary ordinance of God too lightly. For they make it only a badge of our profession, a mark of difference between the true and the false church, a sign of mutual fellowship, a bare sign of spiritual grace, a picture of mortification, regeneration, ingrafting into Christ, and the like, but no more. These indeed are some of the ends and uses of baptism. But in that they confine all its efficacy to these, they take away the greatest comfort and truest benefit which the church reaps by it, as may be gathered from the points noted#footnote[§. 41.] before.

=== §. 46. #emph[Of the inward washing by baptism.] #footnote[#emph[Ecce quia Christus sanctificat; ecce quia Christus ipso lauacro aquae in verbo vbi Minister videtur corporaliter operari ipse abluit, ipse mundat. Aug. contr. lit. Petil. l.] 3. #emph[c.] 49. \[See, it is Christ who sanctifies; see, Christ himself, by the washing of water in the word, where the minister is seen to work bodily, himself washes, himself cleanses.\]]

In that Christ cleanses his church #emph[with] this #emph[washing of water,] I observe that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Whoever are fully baptized are cleansed from sin.]]

#emph[Fully,] that is, powerfully and effectually: inwardly by the Spirit, as well as outwardly by the minister.

#emph[Cleansed,] both from the guilt of sin by Christ’s blood, and from the power of sin by the work of his Spirit.

To this purpose tend the many emphatic phrases which the apostles apply to baptism: that we were “baptized into Christ Jesus,” “baptized into His death,” “buried with Him through baptism”; that “baptism … now saves you#super[+]”; that baptism is “the washing of regeneration,”#footnote[Rom 6:3, 4. 1 Pet 3:21. Titus 3:5.] and the like.

Vain is the rejoicing of many who boast of their baptism,#footnote[Use.] and think themselves by virtue of it to be as good Christians as the best, and yet live and lie in their sin, more smeared and defiled with it than they were when they were first born. John says that Christ baptizes “with the Holy Spirit#footnote[Matt 3:11.] and with fire.” The apostle says that Christ cleanses “with the washing of water.” If that fire of the Holy Spirit does not burn up the dross of sin in you, and this water does not wash away the filth of sin, you were never #emph[fully] baptized. It may be that the hand of some minister has sprinkled a little water on your face, but Christ’s blood has not yet been sprinkled on your soul. All the benefit which you reap from your baptism is that one day you will answer dearly for the abuse of so honorable an ordinance.

=== §. 47. #emph[Of joining the word with baptism.]

The other means of sanctifying and cleansing the church expressed here is the #emph[word.] Since this is applied to baptism and joined with it, it must be meant of the promise of grace sealed up in baptism, which is God’s promise of justifying us freely and sanctifying us effectually, plainly made known and truly believed. Since this means is added to this sacrament in this way, we may well infer that

#emph[It is necessary that the word and baptism go together.] That is,#footnote[Doctrine.] where this sacrament is administered, its doctrine must be taught truly, plainly and understandably, so that its nature, efficacy, end and use may be made known, and the covenant of God sealed up by it may be believed. So Christ says, “Go and teach all nations, baptizing them.”#footnote[Matt 28:19.] So the#footnote[Luke 3:3.] Baptist did, and the#footnote[Acts 2:38; 8:12, 37; 10:47; 16:15, 33.] apostles: they preached the gospel to those whom they baptized.

1\. A sacrament without the word is but an idle ceremony,#footnote[#emph[Reasons. Detrahe verbum & quid est aqua nisi aqua? accedat verbum ad elementum & fit sacramentum.] \[Take away the word, and what is the water but water? Let the word be added to the element, and it becomes a sacrament.\] #emph[Aug. in Joh.] 15. #emph[tract.] 80.] no more than a seal without a covenant. For it is the word that makes known the covenant of God.

2\. It is the word which makes the greatest difference between the sacramental washing of water and ordinary, common washing.

3\. By the word the ordinary creatures which we use are sanctified; much more the holy ordinances of God, of which baptism is one of the chief.

#emph[Question.] Is it not lawful, then, to administer baptism without#footnote[1 Tim 4:5.] a sermon?

#emph[Answer.] It is a very commendable and honorable manner of administering that sacrament to administer it at a time when the word is preached. Yet I do not think a sermon at that time to be so necessary that it would be unlawful to administer baptism without one. For the joining of the word and the sacrament spoken of here is this: that those who are baptized, or who present children to be baptized and answer for them, or who are present at the administering of baptism, or who live in the places where it is usually administered, should be instructed in the gospel, and taught the covenant which baptism seals up. Besides, the liturgy and public form prescribed for the administering of baptism, both in our church and in other Reformed churches, lays down the nature, efficacy, end, use, and other such points belonging to that sacrament, and plainly declares the covenant of God sealed up by it. So in our churches, and others like them, where such forms are prescribed to be always used, the word is never separated from baptism, though there is no sermon at the administering of baptism.

The Church of Rome directly transgresses against the#footnote[Papists do not join the word and the seal.] rule set down above, of joining the word and baptism together. For though they have a public form prescribed, yet since it is in an unknown tongue, not understood by the people nor explained to them, it is all the same as if there were no form at all, no word at all. For what is not understood is all the same#footnote[1 Cor 14:9. #emph[etc.]] as if it were not spoken.

Much more heinous is the transgression of those who live under the gospel, where it is preached plainly to the understanding and capacity of the simplest, and yet are careless in coming to it or in attending to it. So they remain as ignorant as if they lived in places where the word is not preached at all, or is preached in an unknown tongue. Such ignorant persons, if they were not baptized, are not worthy, while they remain so ignorant, to be baptized, nor to present their children to be baptized, nor to be present at the baptism of others. As ministers who baptize ought to preach the word, so those who are baptized ought to be instructed in the word.
